ct, while their sum depends on which magnitude is greater.Use this in signed factoring2. Expand 4(2x − 3).4(2x − 3) = 8x − 12Distribute 4 to both terms. Multiplying only 2x would miss the constant product.Review common factors and distribution3. Simplify 3x² + 5x − x² + 2x.3x2 + 5x − x2 + 2x = 2x2 + 7xCombine matching powers. The x² terms and x terms are two different groups.Review multiplying and collecting termsStart the unitOpen the reference sheetMath 10C · Chapter 3 · Lesson 3.1Factors & multiples of whole numbersUse prime factors to decide what numbers share—and when repeating patterns meet.Learning time35–50 minutes · work at your paceYou will needPaper or a place to show your workingLearning goalDetermine prime factorizations, greatest common factors and least common multiples.What success looks likeI can choose GCF or LCM for a situation and explain why.Automatic checking is paused for this lessonYour existing work is kept and no completion is awarded by pausing. Use the written explanations, printable questions and paper-work route, or contact your teacher. Other available topics can still be practised.Review my work and prepare a help requestHow to complete this lessonFollow the explanation and worked examples. Attempt “Your turn” before opening help, then finish the lesson check and explain one decision. Already confident? Go straight to the lesson check; the teaching stays available.Go to the lesson check · Practise this topic · Print this lessonA factor fits inside. A multiple builds out.Think of 24 objects arranged in equal rows. Six rows of four work because 6 × 4 = 24. That makes both 6 and 4 factors of 24. Seven is not a factor: it does not divide 24 with no remainder.A multiple goes in the other direction. Start with 6 and make whole-number copies: 6, 12, 18, 24, 30, … . There are only finitely many positive factors of 24, but its positive multiples continue without ending.QuestionWhat you are looking forExample“What divides this number?”A factor4 is a factor of 24.“What comes in repeated groups of this number?”A multiple24 is a multiple of 6.These two ideas lead to different problem-solving decisions. Dividing supplies into the greatest number of identical groups calls for a greatest common factor. Finding when two repeating events next coincide calls for a least common multiple.Worked exampleWrite a number as prime building blocksDetermine the prime factorization of 180. A prime number has exactly two positive factors: 1 and itself.Break 180 into convenient factors.180 = 18  ·  10Neither branch is prime yet. Continue each branch, rather than stopping at any product that happens to equal 180.Split until every factor is prime.180 = 2  ·  3  ·  3  ·  2  ·  518 gives 2 × 3 × 3, and 10 gives 2 × 5. The order does not affect the product.Collect repeated factors.180 = 22  ·  32  ·  5Exponent notation records repeated multiplication. Check by multiplying: 4 × 9 × 5 = 180.Why prime factorization helpsEvery composite whole number greater than 1 can be broken into prime factors. Different factor trees can end at the same prime factors in a different order. That gives us a reliable way to compare numbers.18018102 · 3 · 32 · 51 is neither prime nor composite. It has only one positive factor, so it does not belong at the end of a prime factor tree.Worked exampleChoose the shared part—or enough for bothFind the GCF and LCM of 48 and 60.Write both prime factorizations.48 = 24  ·  3;   60 = 22  ·  3  ·  5Write repeated primes carefully. Each exponent tells you how many copies are available.For the GCF, keep only what both numbers contain.GCF = 22  ·  3 = 12Both have at least two 2s and one 3. Only 60 has a 5, so 5 cannot be in a common factor.For the LCM, keep enough prime factors to include each number.LCM = 24  ·  3  ·  5 = 240Use the greatest required number of copies of each prime. Check: 240 ÷ 48 = 5 and 240 ÷ 60 = 4.Check the decision, not just the calculationSuppose 48 pencils and 60 erasers are shared into identical supply bags, with no leftovers. The greatest possible number of bags is 12: the GCF. Each bag gets four pencils and five erasers. By contrast, two routines repeated every 48 and 60 minutes line up again after 240 minutes: the LCM.Your turnFind the greatest common factor of 36 and 54.Your answerType powers as x^2 and multiply with brackets: (x+3)(x+4). For number questions, enter a number or product.Check answerGive me a hintShow worked solutionRefresh a prerequisite, then returnAnother explanation: factors and multiplesOptional video. YouTube loads only when you choose it. Everything needed for this lesson is also explained in writing.Load videoOpen video in a new tabKeep these ideas straightPrime factorA prime number that divides a whole number exactly.Prime factorizationA whole number written as a product of primes.GCFThe greatest positive factor common to the numbers.LCMThe least positive multiple common to the numbers.Show your thinkingPut it togetherTry this without opening a hint first. Then explain one decision. You may use paper and keep that work for your teacher.Lesson checkTwo lights flash every 18 seconds and 24 seconds. They flash together now. After how many seconds will they next flash together? Enter seconds as a number.Your answerType powers as x^2 and multiply with brackets: (x+3)(x+4). For number questions, enter a number or product.Check answerGive me a hintShow worked solutionRefresh a prerequisite, then returnWhy does the flashing-light question need an LCM rather than a GCF?Short explanation · up to 160 characters I explained my method on paper instead.Record this lesson checkMore practice for 3.1Your lesson check has not been recorded.Recording a check tracks work completed, not a grade or a claim of mastery. A response that needs repair is kept with that status.PreviousContinue to 3.2Math 10C · Chapter 3 · Lesson 3.2Perfect squares, perfect cubes & rootsConnect number patterns to the side of a square and the edge of a cube.Learning time35–50 minutes · work at your paceYou will needPaper or a place to show your workingLearning goalRecognize perfect squares and cubes, and determine or estimate their roots.What success looks likeI can choose the correct root and give a dimension with the correct units.Automatic checking is paused for this lessonYour existing work is kept and no completion is awarded by pausing. Use the written explanations, printable questions and paper-work route, or contact your teacher. Other available topics can still be practised.Review my work and prepare a help requestHow to complete this lessonFollow the explanation and worked examples. Attempt “Your turn” before opening help, then finish the lesson check and explain one decision. Already confident? Go straight to the lesson check; the teaching stays available.Go to the lesson check · Practise this topic · Print this lessonRoots undo repeated multiplicationSquaring multiplies a number by itself. Cubing multiplies three copies. A square root or cube root reverses that relationship.Build the resultUndo itGeometric meaning122 = 144√144 = 12A square of area 144 has side length 12.53 = 125∛125 = 5A cube of volume 125 has edge length 5.A perfect square is a whole number that is the square of a whole number. A perfect cube is a whole number that is the cube of a whole number. Learning common examples helps, but prime factorization explains why a number belongs in either group.Worked examplePair the prime factors for a square rootDetermine √900 without first reaching for a calculator.Factor the number.900 = 22  ·  32  ·  52The prime factors can all be grouped into pairs.Take one from each identical pair.30  ·  30 = (2  ·  3  ·  5)(2  ·  3  ·  5)Each side of the multiplication gets one 2, one 3 and one 5.State the principal square root.302 = 900Therefore √900 = 30. The radical symbol asks for the nonnegative square root, not ±30.Pairs for squares. Triples for cubes.In a perfect square’s prime factorization, each exponent is even. In a perfect cube’s prime factorization, each exponent is a multiple of three. This is why checking only the last digit is not a reliable general method.NumberPrime factorizationWhat the groups tell us14424  ·  32All factors pair: it is a perfect square.21623  ·  33All factors form triples: it is a perfect cube.6426Six copies can form pairs or triples: both.7223  ·  32A factor is left over in pairs and in triples: neither.If you are asked which real numbers square to 900, both 30 and −30 work. But √900 by itself means 30. Keep the question and the notation together.Worked exampleUse a root to find a dimensionA cube-shaped container has volume 1,728 cm³. Find its edge length.Match volume to the correct operation.V = s3A cube has three equal dimensions. A square root would answer an area question, not this volume question.Group the prime factors in threes.1728 = 26  ·  33Two groups of three 2s give 2 × 2. One group of three 3s gives 3.Find and check the edge.s = 2  ·  2  ·  3 = 12Check: 12 × 12 × 12 = 1,728. The edge is 12 cm; a length is not measured in cm³.When the number is not a perfect powerYou can still locate a root between two whole numbers. Since 7² = 49 and 8² = 64, √58 lies between 7 and 8. Since 4³ = 64 and 5³ = 125, ∛100 lies between 4 and 5. An estimate should be consistent with these bounds.Do not write √58 = 7 just because 7 is the lower whole number. Use “between 7 and 8,” or the approximation symbol when reporting a decimal estimate.Your turnA cube has volume 343 cm³. What is its edge length? Enter the number of centimetres.Your answerType powers as x^2 and multiply with brackets: (x+3)(x+4). For number questions, enter a number or product.Check answerGive me a hintShow worked solutionRefresh a prerequisite, then returnAnother explanation: squares, cubes and rootsOptional video. YouTube loads only when you choose it. Everything needed for this lesson is also explained in writing.Load videoOpen video in a new tabKeep these ideas straightPerfect squareThe result of squaring a whole number.Principal square rootThe nonnegative number whose square is the given nonnegative number.Perfect cubeThe result of cubing a whole number.Cube rootA number that multiplies by itself three times to give the original number.Show your thinkingPut it togetherTry this without opening a hint first. Then explain one decision. You may use paper and keep that work for your teacher.Lesson checkA square has area 324 m². What is its side length? Enter the number of metres.Your answerType powers as x^2 and multiply with brackets: (x+3)(x+4). For number questions, enter a number or product.Check answerGive me a hintShow worked solutionRefresh a prerequisite, then returnWhy did you use a square root, and why is the final unit metres rather than square metres?Short explanation · up to 160 characters I explained my method on paper instead.Record this lesson checkMore practice for 3.2Your lesson check has not been recorded.Recording a check tracks work completed, not a grade or a claim of mastery. A response that needs repair is kept with that status.PreviousContinue to 3.3Bracket a root before you estimateYou do not need a calculator to locate these roots between consecutive whole numbers. Enter the lower and upper integers, then compare the corresponding powers.Between which consecutive whole numbers does the square root of 58 lie?√58Lower whole numberUpper whole numberCheck answerGive me a hintShow worked solutionBetween which consecutive whole numbers does the cube root of 100 lie?∛100Lower whole numberUpper whole numberCheck answerGive me a hintShow worked solutionMath 10C · Chapter 3 · Lesson 3.3Common factors of a polynomialTurn a sum into a product without losing a sign, a variable or a term.Learning time35–50 minutes · work at your paceYou will needPaper or a place to show your workingLearning goalRemove numerical and variable greatest common factors from polynomials.What success looks likeI can divide every term correctly and verify by expanding.Automatic checking is paused for this lessonYour existing work is kept and no completion is awarded by pausing. Use the written explanations, printable questions and paper-work route, or contact your teacher. Other available topics can still be practised.Review my work and prepare a help requestHow to complete this lessonFollow the explanation and worked examples. Attempt “Your turn” before opening help, then finish the lesson check and explain one decision. Already confident? Go straight to the lesson check; the teaching stays available.Go to the lesson check · Practise this topic · Print this lessonFactoring is distribution in reverseYou already know how to expand 5(x + 4): multiply 5 by every term inside the brackets to get 5x + 20. Factoring reverses that process. The expression stays the same; its form changes from a sum to a product.5x + 20 = 5(x + 4)Before working with trinomials, make common-factor removal a habit. Ask two questions: what numerical factor divides every coefficient, and which variables occur in every term?For a variable, use the smallest exponent shared by all terms. The common variable part of x³ and x² is x², not x³. If one term has no x at all, x is not a factor common to every term.Worked exampleRemove the whole common factorFactor 18x² + 30x.Compare coefficients and variables.GCF = 6xThe GCF of 18 and 30 is 6. Both terms contain x, but only the first has x².Divide every term by 6x.18x2 ÷ 6x = 3x;   30x ÷ 6x = 5The terms remaining inside the brackets must rebuild the original terms when multiplied by 6x.Write a product and verify.18x2 + 30x = 6x(3x + 5)Expand: 6x × 3x = 18x² and 6x × 5 = 30x. Both original terms return.Correct value is not the same as complete factoringWriting 18x² + 30x as 3x(6x + 10) is not false algebra. It expands correctly. But the inside terms still share a factor of 2, so common-factor removal is unfinished.3x(6x + 10) = 6x(3x + 5)A frequent error is losing a term that becomes 1. If you take 7x out of 7x² + 7x, the second term becomes 1—not zero and not nothing.7x2 + 7x = 7x(x + 1)The check is multiplication, not appearance. A tidy-looking pair of brackets is useful only if it expands to every original term.Worked exampleKeep signs and both variablesRemove the GCF from −18x²y − 12xy².Find the common numerical and variable parts.GCF = 6xyBoth coefficients contain 6, and both terms contain at least one x and one y.Choose a convenient sign.−18x2 · y − 12x · y2 = −6xy(3x + 2y)Taking out −6xy leaves positive terms inside. An equivalent correctly signed form is also valid.Distribute the entire outside factor.(−6xy)(3x) + (−6xy)(2y)These products are −18x²y and −12xy². The variables and signs both match the original.Try it before looking at the worked solutionYour turnRemove the greatest common factor.12x2+18xYour answerType powers as x^2 and multiply with brackets: (x+3)(x+4). For number questions, enter a number or product.Check answerGive me a hintShow worked solutionRefresh a prerequisite, then returnFor higher powers, the same rule still applies. For example, 8x⁴ − 12x² has GCF 4x², leaving 4x²(2x² − 3). You are comparing what every term contains; the number of terms does not change that rule.Another explanation: common-factor removalOptional video. YouTube loads only when you choose it. Everything needed for this lesson is also explained in writing.Load videoOpen video in a new tabKeep these ideas straightPolynomialA sum of terms with whole-number exponents on its variables.TermA part of an expression separated by addition or subtraction.CoefficientThe numerical multiplier of the variable part of a term.Factor completelyWrite the requested product with no further required factoring left.Show your thinkingPut it togetherTry this without opening a hint first. Then explain one decision. You may use paper and keep that work for your teacher.Lesson checkRemove the greatest common factor from every term.−12x2 · y−20x · y2Your answerType powers as x^2 and multiply with brackets: (x