resents: the small pieces are the terms of the expanded polynomial; the outside lengths are its binomial factors.Explore the two side lengthsChange the positive constants below. Watch the two x-regions and the constant rectangle. The diagram is schematic: the x-region represents a variable length, not a fixed measurement.Area model · (x + p)(x + q)p3q4x4x²4x3x12(x + 3)(x + 4) = x² + 7x + 12The x strips add to 7x. The corner has area 12.The rows have heights x and p; the columns have widths x and q. The four areas are x², qx, px and pq. This positive-length model is not used to pretend a physical length is negative.Worked exampleRead the factors from the rectangleA collection contains one x² region, 8x in strips, and 15 unit squares. Find binomial factors.Look for a rectangle for the 15 unit squares.15 = 1  ·  15 = 3  ·  5The two constant dimensions must multiply to 15.Match the x-strips as well.3x + 5x = 8xDimensions 1 and 15 would need 16x of strips, not 8x. The constant product alone is not enough.Write the side lengths.x2 + 8x + 15 = (x + 3)(x + 5)Each full side contains an x length and a constant length. Multiplication checks both the middle term and constant.Not every set makes the required rectangleConsider x² + 5x + 5. Integer side additions would have to multiply to 5. The positive pair 1 and 5 adds to 6, not 5. That set cannot make the required binomial rectangle with whole-number side additions. Do not add a tile just to force an answer: that would change the polynomial.For negative terms later, we will use signed algebraic products. A negative coefficient is not a negative physical area. Keeping that distinction prevents the model from telling a misleading story.Your turnExpand the rectangle’s two side lengths and combine like terms.(x+4)(x+5)Your answerType powers as x^2 and multiply with brackets: (x+3)(x+4). For number questions, enter a number or product.Check answerGive me a hintShow worked solutionRefresh a prerequisite, then returnKeep these ideas 