aight to the lesson check; the teaching stays available.Go to the lesson check · Practise this topic · Print this lessonUse both conditions—not just oneFor a trinomial whose leading coefficient is 1, expansion gives a useful pattern.(x + p)(x + q) = x2 + (p + q)x + pqTo factor x² + bx + c, look for integers p and q that multiply to c and add to b. “Monic” means the leading coefficient is 1. This sum/product rule depends on that leading 1; a different leading coefficient needs the method in 3.6.Write the pair you are trying, then check both conditions. You are not guessing a full answer blindly—you are narrowing a small list of factor pairs.Worked exampleBuild a positive factor pairFactor x² + 11x + 24.List factor pairs for the constant.1  ·  24;  2  ·  12;  3  ·  8;  4  ·  6The signed product must be 24. Since the middle term is positive, start with positive pairs.Choose the pair with sum 11.3 + 8 = 11The other sums are 25, 14 and 10. Only 3 and 8 satisfy both conditions.Write and expand the factors.x2 + 11x + 24 = (x + 3)(x + 8)The middle products are 8x and 3x; they combine to 11x.Make sign decisions before searchingConstant cMiddle coefficient bSigns in the factorsPositivePositiveBoth positivePositiveNegativeBoth negativeNegativeEitherOpposite signs; the greater magnitude sets the sign of the sum.For x² − 9x + 20, the product is positive and sum negative: use −4 and −5. For x² + x − 20, use 5 and −4. The same magnitudes can create different trinomials because the signs change the sum and product.Worked exampleFactor a trinomial with opposite signsFactor x² − 2x − 35.Use opposite signs because the product is negative.(−7)(5) = −35The larger magnitude must be negative to produce a negative middle coefficient.Check the signed sum.−7 + 5 = −2This matches the coefficient of x.Write and verify the answer.x2 − 2x − 35 = (x − 7)(x + 5)Expanding gives x² + 5x − 7x − 35, which simplifies to the original.Show why your factor pair worksOne legitimate method is to split the middle term, group the terms, and remove the shared binomial. For x² + 7x + 12, enter the full intermediate expression—not just two numbers.Build an intermediate stepSplit the middle term into two terms that will allow grouping. Keep the complete expression.x2+7x+12Your answerType powers as x^2 and multiply with brackets: (x+3)(x+4). For number questions, enter a number or product.Check answerGive me a hintShow worked solutionRefresh a prerequisite, then returnThe order of the two middle terms can change without changing the method. Another valid strategy can be kept on paper and checked using its final factors. You do not have to reproduce a preferred sequence simply to show that your mathematics works.Worked exampleDo not stop after taking out the GCFFactor 3x² + 21x + 36 completely.Remove the numerical GCF.3x2 + 21x + 36 = 3(x2 + 7x + 12)This is equivalent, but the trinomial inside can still be factored.Factor the inside monic trinomial.x2 + 7x + 12 = (x + 3)(x + 4)The pair multiplies to 12 and adds to 7.Keep the outside factor.3x2 + 21x + 36 = 3(x + 3)(x + 4)Dropping the 3 would change every coefficient. Verify the entire product.What went wrong?Repair an answerA student wrote (x + 2)(x + 6). Repair the factorization of the polynomial below.x2+7x+12Your answerType powers as x^2 and multiply with brackets: (x+3)(x+4). For number questions, enter a number or product.Check answerGive me a hintShow worked solutionRefresh a prerequisite, then returnAnother explanation: factoring monic trinomialsOptional video. YouTube loads only when you choose it. Everything needed for this lesson is also explained in writing.Load videoOpen video in a new tabKeep these ideas straightMonicA polynomial with leading coefficient 1.Leading coefficientThe coefficient of the highest-degree term.DecompositionSplitting a term into equivalent parts to make grouping possible.Equivalent expressionsExpressions with the same value for every allowed value of the variables.Try it with less help each