he same binomial.Factor out that whole binomial.(3x + 2)(2x + 3)Expanding gives 6x² + 9x + 4x + 6, as required.What the two middle products must doFor (ux + v)(wx + z), the middle terms are uzx and vwx. Their coefficients add to b. Their product is (uz)(vw) = (uw)(vz) = ac. That is why decomposition uses ac rather than c alone.Try the decomposition checkpointSplit the middle term into two terms suitable for grouping.6x2+11x+3Your answerType powers as x^2 and multiply with brackets: (x+3)(x+4). For number questions, enter a number or product.Check answerGive me a hintShow worked solutionRefresh a prerequisite, then returnIf the two bracketed expressions do not match after grouping, check your signs and the factor you removed. Do not change one bracket merely to make it look like the other.Worked exampleGroup correctly when one group is negativeFactor 6x² − x − 2.Find a pair for product −12 and sum −1.−4 + 3 = −1;   (−4)(3) = −12The signs are opposite because the constant is negative.Split and group.6x2 − 4x + 3x − 2 = 2x(3x − 2) + 1(3x − 2)The common factor of the second group is 1. Keeping that 1 makes the last step visible.Remove the repeated binomial.6x2 − x − 2 = (2x + 1)(3x − 2)Both (2x + 1)(3x − 2) and the factors in reverse order are valid.Start with the common factor; finish by checkingFor 12x² + 26x + 12, first remove 2. Then factor the inside expression using the first worked example to get 2(3x + 2)(2x + 3). This keeps the pair search smaller and prevents an incomplete final product.If no integer pair satisfies the required product and sum after the common factor is removed, do not force a pair. In the integer-factor setting, not every quadratic splits into integer binomials. Verify a possible answer by expanding, not by its resemblance to a familiar pattern.Another explanation: factoring when the leading coefficient is not oneOptional video. YouTube loads only when you choose it. Everything needed for this lesson is also explained in writing.Load videoOpe