confident? Go straight to the lesson check; the teaching stays available.Go to the lesson check · Practise this topic · Print this lessonDistribution works beyond two binomialsMultiplying two binomials produces four partial products. That is only one case of the distributive property. A binomial times a trinomial produces six partial products; two trinomials produce nine.Use a table or write one distribution line for each term. A reliable method helps you account for every product before combining like terms. “FOIL” describes a two-by-two arrangement, but it is not a complete method for every polynomial product.One factorOther factorPartial products to account for2 terms2 terms42 terms3 terms63 terms3 terms9Worked exampleMultiply a binomial by a trinomialExpand and simplify (x + 2)(x² + 3x − 4).Distribute x and 2 separately.x(x2 + 3x − 4) + 2(x2 + 3x − 4)Every term in the second factor is multiplied twice: once by x and once by 2.Write all six products.x3 + 3x2 − 4x + 2x2 + 6x − 8Multiplying powers of the same variable adds exponents: x × x² = x³.Collect only like terms.x3 + 5x2 + 2x − 8Combine the x² terms and the x terms separately. A cubic term cannot combine with a quadratic term.Use a table to keep track×x²3x−4xx³3x²−4x22x²6x−8Read every cell once, then collect. The table is an accounting tool, not a different rule. For negative products, it is a signed multiplication table—not a claim that physical side lengths are negative.Your turnExpand and simplify.(x+3)(x2−x−2)Your answerType powers as x^2 and multiply with brackets: (x+3)(x+4). For number questions, enter a number or product.Check answerGive me a hintShow worked solutionRefresh a prerequisite, then returnWorked exampleMultiply with two variablesExpand (2x + 3y)(x − 4y).Distribute each term.2x(x − 4y) + 3y(x − 4y)Each product must keep both variable parts where they appear.Write the four products.2x2 − 8xy + 3xy − 12y2The terms −8xy and 3xy are like terms because their variable parts match exactly.Collect the matching terms.2x2 − 5xy − 12y2Neither x² nor y² combines with xy. Variable letters and exponents both matter.Worked exampleApply a polynomial product to volumeA rectangular prism has base area x² + 2x + 3 and height x + 1. Write a simplified volume expression.Use the geometric relationship.V = (x2 + 2x + 3)(x + 1)Volume is base area times height, so the whole area expression is multiplied by the whole height expression.Distribute all terms.V = x3 + 2x2 + 3x + x2 + 2x + 3The two groups correspond to multiplying the area by x and by 1.Collect like terms and interpret.V = x3 + 3x2 + 5x + 3If the given lengths are in centimetres, this is a volume in cubic centimetres, for values making the dimensions meaningful.Use checks that actually test the workExpansion can be checked against the table of partial products. Substituting a convenient number can also catch some errors. However, agreement at one number is not a proof that two polynomials are identical.For example, x² + 5x + 6 and x² + 7x + 6 agree at x = 0 even though the middle coefficients are different. Your final justification is the correct distribution and collection of every term.Another explanation: multiplying polynomialsOptional video. YouTube loads only when you choose it. Everything needed for this lesson is also explained in writing.Load videoOpen video in a new tabKeep these ideas straightLike termsTerms with exactly the same variable part and exponents.Partial productOne product formed by selecting a term from each factor.Degree of a termThe sum of the variable exponents in that term.Expand and simplifyDistribute all products and collect like terms.Show your thinkingPut it togetherTry this without opening a hint first. Then explain one decision. You may use paper and keep that work for your teacher.Lesson checkExpand and simplify.(2x−y)(x+3y)Your answerType powers as x^2 and multiply with brackets: (x+3)(x+4). For number questions, enter a number or product.Check answerGive me a hintShow worked sol