ampleCheck the middle term of a perfect squareFactor 4x² + 20x + 25.Identify the square roots of the end terms.4x2 = (2x)2;   25 = 52These suggest a binomial involving 2x and 5.Test twice their product.2(2x)(5) = 20xThe required middle term matches. Square end terms alone would not establish the pattern.Use the repeated binomial factor.4x2 + 20x + 25 = (2x + 5)2Writing (2x + 5)(2x + 5) is also correct. Expansion confirms the middle term.A squared binomial has cross productsFind and repair the omitted termsA student wrote (x + 5)² = x² + 25. Expand correctly to repair the missing terms.(x+5)2Your answerType powers as x^2 and multiply with brackets: (x+3)(x+4). For number questions, enter a number or product.Check answerGive me a hintShow worked solutionRefresh a prerequisite, then returnCompare x² + 8x + 16 with x² + 6x + 16. Only the first is (x + 4)², because twice x times 4 is 8x. Looking at the first and last terms without checking the middle term is not enough.Your turnFactor the perfect square trinomial.9x2−24x+16Your answerType powers as x^2 and multiply with brackets: (x+3)(x+4). For number questions, enter a number or product.Check answerGive me a hintShow worked solutionRefresh a prerequisite, then returnWorked exampleFactor a difference of squaresFactor 49x² − 36y².Check both square terms and the subtraction.49x2 = (7x)2;   36y2 = (6y)2There are two terms, and the operation is subtraction.Use opposite signs.49x2 − 36y2 = (7x − 6y)(7x + 6y)The mixed products −42xy and +42xy cancel.Verify the cancellation.49x2 + 42xy − 42xy − 36y2There is no middle term left. This is what creates the difference-of-squares pattern.Worked exampleContinue when a factor is still factorableFactor 3x⁴ − 48 completely over the integers.Take out the GCF first.3x4 − 48 = 3(x4 − 16)The remaining binomial is a difference of squares.Factor the difference.3(x2 − 4)(x2 + 4)This uses x⁴ = (x²)² and 16 = 4².Check each factor again.3(x − 2)(x + 2)(x2 + 4)x² − 4 factors again. x² + 4 is a sum of squares and does not factor further over the integers.Use a consistent factoring orderFirst: remove a numerical and variable GCF. Next: count terms and look for a known pattern. Then: use a signed pair or decomposition if the trinomial is not a special square. Finally: inspect each factor and expand to check the whole answer.For two-variable trinomials, the same decomposition reasoning can apply. For example, 2x² − 5xy + 2y² = (2x − y)(x − 2y). The middle products −4xy and −xy add to −5xy. A variable in the constant position does not remove the need to check both products.Do not apply (a − b)(a + b) to a² + b². The product creates subtraction, so it would change the expression.Another explanation: special factoring patternsOptional video. YouTube loads only when you choose it. Everything needed for this lesson is also explained in writing.Load videoOpen video in a new tabKeep these ideas straightPerfect square trinomialA trinomial formed by squaring a binomial.Difference of squaresA² − B², which factors as (A − B)(A + B).Repeated factorA factor that occurs more than once, often written using an exponent.VerifyUse expansion to establish that the factored and original forms match.Show your thinkingPut it togetherTry this without opening a hint first. Then explain one decision. You may use paper and keep that work for your teacher.Lesson checkFactor completely over the integers.2x2−18Your answerType powers as x^2 and multiply with brackets: (x+3)(x+4). For number questions, enter a number or product.Check answerGive me a hintShow worked solutionRefresh a prerequisite, then returnExplain why the outside factor must remain and why the two binomials use opposite signs.Short explanation · up to 160 characters I explained my method on paper instead.Record this lesson checkMore practice for 3.8Your lesson check has not been recorded.Recording a check tracks work completed, not a grade or a claim of mastery. A response that needs repair is kept with that status.PreviousCo