stions from all eight sections. Keep written steps on paper when that works better for your mathematics. You do not need to transcribe every line back into this site.Print unit study packOpen the reference sheetFor a single lesson, use “Print this lesson” in its completion guide. Your browser can print to paper or save a PDF.Optional videosOptional video explanations sit beside the relevant teaching. They load from YouTube only when selected. If a video is unavailable or blocked, the written lesson and worked solutions remain usable.Your course workbookAdditional workbook pages, official assessments and submission instructions remain in your Brightspace course. This unit does not replace your teacher’s assessment directions.Saving and moving workThe standalone preview saves on this browser and device. Keep a process report for selected working. A backup can move your current unit record, but a backup is not an assignment submission.Download work backupRestore work backupRestoring a backup asks before replacing the current unit record. Never restore another learner’s file on a shared device.Reference · written supportWorked support libraryOpen only the example you need. Reading support for an identical problem is recorded when you use that problem again.3.1 · Find the greatest common factor of 36 and 54. List the prime factors shared by both numbers. Keep only the copies they both have.36 = 2 × 2 × 3 × 3; 54 = 2 × 3 × 3 × 3.The shared part is 2 × 3 × 3 = 18.3.1 · Two lights flash every 18 seconds and 24 seconds. They flash together now. After how many seconds will they next flash together? Enter seconds as a number. You need the first shared multiple, not the greatest shared factor.18 = 2 × 3² and 24 = 2³ × 3.The LCM needs 2³ × 3² = 72, so the next simultaneous flash is after 72 seconds.3.2 · A cube has volume 343 cm³. What is its edge length? Enter the number of centimetres. Which whole number multiplied by itself three times equals 343?343 = 7 × 7 × 7.The cube root of 343 is 7. The edge length is 7 cm.3.2 · A square has area 324 m². What is its side length? Enter the number of metres. A square root reverses squaring. Try a side length near 20 m.324 = 2² × 3⁴ = (2 × 3²)².√324 = 18. Check: 18 × 18 = 324. The side length is 18 m.3.3 · Remove the greatest common factor. 12x^2+18xThe numerical GCF is 6. Both terms also contain x.12x² ÷ 6x = 2x and 18x ÷ 6x = 3.12x² + 18x = 6x(2x + 3).3.3 · Remove the greatest common factor from every term. -12x^2*y-20x*y^2Both terms contain 4xy. Taking out −4xy makes the inside terms positive.The GCF of 12 and 20 is 4. The common variable part is xy.−12x²y − 20xy² = −4xy(3x + 5y).Expand −4xy into both terms to verify the original expression.3.4 · Expand the rectangle’s two side lengths and combine like terms. (x+4)(x+5)Find all four smaller areas before combining the x terms.x × x = x²; x × 5 = 5x; 4 × x = 4x; 4 × 5 = 20.x² + 5x + 4x + 20 = x² + 9x + 20.3.4 · A rectangle has the area below. Factor it to give its side-length expressions. x^2+9x+14Find a rectangle arrangement whose constant parts multiply to 14 and whose x strips total 9x.2 × 7 = 14 and 2 + 7 = 9.The side lengths are x + 2 and x + 7.(x + 2)(x + 7) expands to x² + 9x + 14.3.5 · Split the middle term into two terms that will allow grouping. Keep the complete expression. x^2+7x+12The two coefficients must add to 7 and multiply to 12.3 + 4 = 7 and 3 × 4 = 12.A useful split is x² + 3x + 4x + 12.Then x(x + 3) + 4(x + 3) = (x + 3)(x + 4).3.5 · Factor completely over the integers. x^2-x-12The constant is negative, so use opposite signs. The signed sum must be −1.−4 × 3 = −12 and −4 + 3 = −1.x² − x − 12 = (x − 4)(x + 3).The middle products are 3x and −4x, which add to −x.3.6 · Split the middle term into two terms suitable for grouping. 6x^2+11x+3Multiply a × c = 6 × 3. Find two numbers with product 18 and sum 11.9 × 2 = 18 and 9 + 2 = 11.6x² + 9x + 2x + 3 preserves the expression.3x(2x + 3) + 1(2x + 3) = (3x + 1)(2x + 3).3.6 · Factor completely over the integers. 6x^2-5x-6Use a × c = −36. A useful pair adds to −5.−9 and 4 multiply to −36 and add to −5.6x² − 9x + 4x − 6 = 3x(2x − 3) + 2(2x − 3).The factors are (3x + 2)(2x − 3).3.7 · Expand and simplify. (x+3)(x^2-x-2)There should be six partial products before you collect like terms.x(x² − x − 2) + 3(x² − x − 2).x³ − x² − 2x + 3x² − 3x − 6.Collect to get x³ + 2x² − 5x − 6.3.7 · Expand and simplify. (2x-y)(x+3y)Multiply each of the two terms in the first factor by each term in the second.2x × x = 2x²; 2x × 3y = 6xy.−y × x = −xy; −y × 3y = −3y².6xy − xy = 5xy, giving 2x² + 5xy − 3y².3.8 · Factor the perfect square trinomial. 9x^2-24x+16Check the square roots of the end terms and twice their product.9x² = (3x)² and 16 = 4².The middle term is −2(3x)(4) = −24x.The trinomial is (3x − 4)².3.8 · Factor completely over the integers. 2x^2-18Take out the common factor before applying the difference-of-squares pattern.2x² − 18 = 2(x² − 9).x² − 9 = (x − 3)(x + 3).The complete answer is 2(x − 3)(x + 3).3.5 · A student wrote (x + 2)(x + 6). Repair the factorization of the polynomial below. x^2+7x+12The student got the constant product right, but 2 + 6 is 8, not 7.Expanding the student’s answer gives x² + 8x + 12.Use 3 and 4: they add to 7 and multiply to 12.The repaired answer is (x + 3)(x + 4).3.8 · A student wrote (x + 5)² = x² + 25. Expand correctly to repair the missing terms. (x+5)^2A square means multiply two whole binomials. Find the two cross products.(x + 5)² = (x + 5)(x + 5).x² + 5x + 5x + 25 = x² + 10x + 25.3.1 · Find the greatest common factor of 24 and 36. Enter its value. Keep only the prime-factor copies shared by both numbers.12 divides both 24 and 36.The greatest shared positive factor is 12.3.1 · Find the least common multiple of 30 and 42. Enter its value. Choose enough copies of each prime factor to include both numbers.Use all necessary prime factors to include both 30 and 42.210 ÷ 30 = 7 and 210 ÷ 42 = 5; the LCM is 210.3.1 · Write the prime factorization of 36. Continue splitting any composite factor; every final factor must be prime.36 = 2 × 2 × 3 × 3The prime factors may appear in a different order.3.1 · Find the greatest common factor of 45 and 75. Enter its value. Keep only the prime-factor copies shared by both numbers.15 divides both 45 and 75.The greatest shared positive factor is 15.3.1 · Find the least common multiple of 48 and 72. Enter its value. Choose enough copies of each prime factor to include both numbers.Use all necessary prime factors to include both 48 and 72.144 ÷ 48 = 3 and 144 ÷ 72 = 2; the LCM is 144.3.1 · Write the prime factorization of 28. Continue splitting any composite factor; every final factor must be prime.28 = 2 × 2 × 7The prime factors may appear in a different order.3.1 · Find the greatest common factor of 32 and 48. Enter its value. Keep only the prime-factor copies shared by both numbers.16 divides both 32 and 48.The greatest shared positive factor is 16.3.1 · Find the least common multiple of 18 and 30. Enter its value. Choose enough copies of each prime factor to include both numbers.Use all necessary prime factors to include both 18 and 30.90 ÷ 18 = 5 and 90 ÷ 30 = 3; the LCM is 90.3.1 · Write the prime factorization of 42. Continue splitting any composite factor; every final factor must be prime.42 = 2 × 3 × 7The prime factors may appear in a different order.3.1 · Find the greatest common factor of 60 and 84. Enter its value. Keep only the prime-factor copies shared by both numbers.12 divides both 60 and 84.The greatest shared positive factor is 12.3.1 · Find the least common multiple of 40 and 64. Enter its value. Choose enough copies of each prime factor to include both numbers.Use all necessary prime factors to include both 40 and 64.320 ÷ 40 = 8 and 320 ÷ 64 = 5; the LCM is 320.3.1 · Write the prime factorization of 54. Continue splitting any composite factor; every final factor must be prime.54 = 2 × 3 × 3 × 3The prime factors may appear in a different order.3.2 · Determine the principal square root of 81. Enter its value. Use groups of three prime factors for a cube root, or pairs for a square root.81 = 9 × 9The requested root is 9.3.2 · Determine the cube root of 1728. Enter its value. Use groups of three prime factors for a cube root, or pairs for a square root.1728 = 12 × 12 × 12The requested root is 12.3.2 · Determine the principal square root of 225. Enter its value. Use groups of three prime factors for a cube root, or pairs for a square root.225 = 15 × 15The requested root is 15.3.2 · Determine the principal square root of 324. Enter its value. Use groups of three prime factors for a cube root, or pairs for a square root.324 = 18 × 18The requested root is 18.3.2 · Determine the cube root of 9261. Enter its value. Use groups of three prime factors for a cube root, or pairs for a square root.9261 = 21 × 21 × 21The requested root is 21.3.2 · Determine the principal square root of 64. Enter its value. Use groups of three prime factors for a cube root, or pairs for a square root.64 = 8 × 8The requested root is 8.3.2 · Determine the principal square root of 36. Enter its value. Use groups of three prime factors for a cube root, or pairs for a square root.36 = 6 × 6The requested root is 6.3.2 · Determine the cube root of 1331. Enter its value. Use groups of three prime factors for a cube root, or pairs for a square root.1331 = 11 × 11 × 11The requested root is 11.3.2 · Determine the principal square root of 196. Enter its value. Use groups of three prime factors for a cube root, or pairs for a square root.196 = 14 × 14The requested root is 14.3.2 · Determine the principal square root of 256. Enter its value. Use groups of three prime factors for a cube root, or pairs for a square root.256 = 16 × 16The requested root is 16.3.2 · Determine the cube root of 343. Enter its value. Use groups of three prime factors for a cube root, or pairs for a square root.343 = 7 × 7 × 7The requested root is 7.3.2 · Determine the principal square root of 100. Enter its value. Use groups of three prime factors for a cube root, or pairs for a square root.100 = 10 × 10The requested root is 10.3.3 · Remove the greatest common factor. 6x^2+10xCompare 6 and 10, then find the smallest shared power of x.The common factor is 2x.Dividing the two terms gives 3x and 5.The product is 2x(3x+5). Verify by distributing.3.3 · Remove the greatest common factor. 12x^2+21xCompare 12 and 21, then find the smallest shared power of x.The common factor is 3x.Dividing the two terms gives 4x and 7.The product is 3x(4x+7). Verify by distributing.3.3 · Remove the greatest common factor. 8x^2+12xCompare 8 and 12, then find the smallest shared power of x.The common factor is 4x.Dividing the two terms gives 2x and 3.The product is 4x(2x+3). Verify by distributing.3.3 · Remove the greatest common factor. 15x^2+20xCompare 15 and 20, then find the smallest shared power of x.The common factor is 5x.Dividing the two terms gives 3x and 4.The product is 5x(3x+4). Verify by distributing.3.3 · Remove the greatest common factor. 6x^2+42xCompare 6 and 42, then find the smallest shared power of x.The common factor is 6x.Dividing the two terms gives 1x and 7.The product is 6x(1x+7). Verify by distributing.3.3 · Remove the greatest common factor. 14x^2+35xCompare 14 and 35, then find the smallest shared power of x.The common factor is 7x.Dividing the two terms gives 2x and 5.The product is 7x(2x+5). Verify by distributing.3.3 · Remove the greatest common factor. 15x^2-6xCompare 15 and 6, then find the smallest shared power of x.The common factor is 3x.Dividing the two terms gives 5x and -2.The product is 3x(5x-2). Verify by distributing.3.3 · Remove the greatest common factor. 12x^2-28xCompare 12 and 28, then find the smallest shared power of x.The common factor is 4x.Dividing the two terms gives 3x and -7.The product is 4x(3x-7). Verify by distributing.3.3 · Remove the greatest common factor. 10x^2+18xCompare 10 and 18, then find the smallest 